{"source":"I used to think the quiet part of a meeting meant nobody had an idea. At the archive, people often need a minute to look at the photograph before they talk about it. The first time I helped lead a session, I kept jumping in with background details because I worried the silence meant I had lost everyone. A colleague let the pause run, and then someone pointed out a detail in the window that none of us had noticed.

Now I try not to fill every pause. It doesn't always work; when I'm nervous, I still start explaining too soon. But I've learned that waiting can be part of listening. Sometimes people are finding the words, and sometimes they just need another moment with the picture. I still have to remind myself that a quiet room isn't necessarily an empty one.","candidate":"I used to think the quiet part of a meeting meant nobody had an idea. At the archive, people often need a minute to look at the photograph before they talk about it. The first time I helped lead a session, I kept jumping in with background details because I worried the silence meant I had lost everyone. A colleague let the pause run, and then someone pointed out a detail in the window that none of us had noticed.

